<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<ScrollView xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:background="#F5F5DC"
    android:padding="16dp">

    <LinearLayout
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:orientation="vertical">

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvTitle"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="Intermediate Lesson 1: Divisibility of Integers 1"
            android:textSize="26sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:textColor="#3E2723"
            android:gravity="center"
            android:layout_marginBottom="10dp"/>

        <Button
            android:id="@+id/btnReadLesson"
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_gravity="center"
            android:text="Read Lesson Aloud"
            android:backgroundTint="#8D6E63"
            android:textColor="#FFFFFF"
            android:layout_marginBottom="20dp" />

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvSubtitle1"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="1. Fundamentals of Divisibility"
            android:textSize="20sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:textColor="#5D4037"
            android:layout_marginBottom="8dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvContent1"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="An integer 'a' divides an integer 'b' (written as a | b) if there exists an integer 'k' such that b = a * k. \nImportant properties:\n1. If a | b and b | c, then a | c (Transitivity).\n2. If a | b and a | c, then a | (b + c) and a | (b - c).\n3. More generally, if a | b and a | c, then a perfectly divides any linear combination: a | (mb + nc) for any integers m and n. This is the most used property in Olympiad number theory!"
            android:textSize="16sp"
            android:textColor="#212121"
            android:layout_marginBottom="16dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvSubtitle2"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="2. The Euclidean Division"
            android:textSize="20sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:textColor="#5D4037"
            android:layout_marginBottom="8dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvContent2"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="For any integer 'a' and any positive integer 'b', there exist unique integers 'q' (quotient) and 'r' (remainder) such that:\na = b * q + r\nwhere 0 ≤ r &lt; b. \nIf r = 0, then b perfectly divides a. Understanding remainders is the gateway to modular arithmetic, which we will study in later lessons."
            android:textSize="16sp"
            android:textColor="#212121"
            android:layout_marginBottom="16dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvSubtitle3"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            text="3. The Euclidean Algorithm"
            android:textSize="20sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:textColor="#5D4037"
            android:layout_marginBottom="8dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvContent3"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="To find the Greatest Common Divisor (GCD) of two large numbers, factoring them is too slow. The Euclidean Algorithm uses a brilliant trick: GCD(a, b) = GCD(b, r), where r is the remainder of a divided by b.\nExample: GCD(252, 105):\n252 = 2 * 105 + 42 (So, GCD(252, 105) = GCD(105, 42))\n105 = 2 * 42 + 21 (So, GCD = GCD(42, 21))\n42 = 2 * 21 + 0. Since the remainder is 0, the GCD is 21!"
            android:textSize="16sp"
            android:textColor="#212121"
            android:layout_marginBottom="16dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvSubtitle4"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="4. GCD and LCM Relationship"
            android:textSize="20sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:textColor="#5D4037"
            android:layout_marginBottom="8dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvContent4"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="The Least Common Multiple (LCM) and Greatest Common Divisor (GCD) of two positive integers 'a' and 'b' are beautifully connected by the formula:\nGCD(a, b) * LCM(a, b) = a * b.\nIf two numbers are 'coprime' (meaning their GCD is 1), then their LCM is simply their product!"
            android:textSize="16sp"
            android:textColor="#212121"
            android:layout_marginBottom="20dp"/>

        <View
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="2dp"
            android:background="#D2B48C"
            android:layout_marginBottom="20dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvProblem1Title"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="Olympiad Problem 1: Linear Combinations"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:textSize="18sp"
            android:textColor="#5D4037"/>

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvProblem1Text"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="Prove that for any integer n, the fraction (21n + 4) / (14n + 3) is always in its simplest form (irreducible)."
            android:textSize="16sp"
            android:textColor="#212121"/>

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/tvSolution1"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="Solution: A fraction is irreducible if the GCD of its numerator and denominator is 1. Let d = GCD(21n + 4, 14n + 3).\nBy our divisibility properties, 'd' must divide any linear combination of these two numbers.\nLet's eliminate 'n'. Multiply the first by 2, and the second by 3:\nd | 2(21n + 4) => d | 42n + 8\nd | 3(14n + 3) => d | 42n + 9\nSince 'd' divides both, it must divide their difference:\nd | (42n + 9) - (42n + 8) => d | 1.\nSince the only positive integer that divides 1 is 1, the GCD is 1. The fraction is always irreducible!"
            android:textSize="16sp"
            android:textStyle="italic"
            android:textColor="#2E7D32"
            android:layout_marginTop="4dp"
            android:layout_marginBottom="24dp"/>

        <Button
            android:id="@+id/btnStartTest"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="60dp"
            android:text="START ADVANCED TEST 1"
            android:textSize="18sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:backgroundTint="#5D4037"
            android:textColor="#FFFFFF"
            android:layout_marginBottom="30dp"/>

    </LinearLayout>
</ScrollView>